<figure class="core-demand" aria-label="Two plans returning the same five jobs">
 <div class="plans">
  <section><h3>Without the ordering index</h3><ol><li><strong>Scan</strong><span>Read 50,000 candidate rows</span></li><li><strong>Top-N sort</strong><span>Compare every candidate; retain the best five</span></li><li><strong>Limit</strong><span>Return five rows</span></li></ol><p class="cost">The first answer needs the last candidate to be checked.</p></section>
  <section><h3>With (priority, job_id)</h3><ol><li><strong>Ordered index scan</strong><span>Request the next entry in answer order</span></li><li><strong>Visibility check</strong><span>Produce the next eligible row</span></li><li><strong>Limit</strong><span>Stop after five output rows</span></li></ol><p class="cost">In this fixture, the scan produces five rows and stops.</p></section>
 </div>
 <figcaption>Both plans return jobs 101, 202, 303, 404 and 505. The difference is how much input must be examined before those five can be known. Additional filters or invisible versions can make an ordered scan visit more entries.</figcaption>
</figure>
